    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">1</span>The project in one minute</h2>
<p class="lead">DDX-ES reads a patient's answers to a symptom questionnaire, proposes the most likely diseases, decides
  how urgently the patient needs care, and can show the rule, the facts and the publication behind every one of
  these conclusions.</p>
{{ analogy("A careful junior doctor with a handbook in which every page cites its source. The doctor asks questions,
  thinks of the diseases that could explain the answers, rules out those that cannot, and when you ask “why?”, opens
  the handbook at the right page.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <div class="overview">
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="node cream"><b>Patient's answers</b>age, sex, symptoms, risk factors and history</div>
      <div class="node"><b>Knowledge graph</b>49 diseases · 223 questions · {{ n_rules }} cited rules ·
        calibration counts · OWL 2 RL reasoning</div>
    </div>
    {{ arr() }}
    <div class="node blue center" style="align-self:center;padding:14pt 9pt"><b>Inference engine</b>four phases:
      chain the rules, rule out, weigh the evidence, decide urgency</div>
    {{ arr() }}
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="node white"><b>Ranked diseases</b>the differential diagnosis</div>
      <div class="node white"><b>Triage level</b><span class="lvl e">Emergency</span> to <span class="lvl s">Self-care</span></div>
      <div class="node white"><b>Next steps</b>tests and advice, each with its source</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="node green" style="margin-top:8pt"><b>Explanation for every output</b>HOW was it concluded (a proof
    down to the answers) · WHY is this question asked · WHY NOT another disease · a provenance record in the graph</div>
  <figcaption>Figure 1. DDX-ES at a glance. Everything the system knows is in one knowledge graph; everything it
    concludes can be explained.</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
  <li><b>Input:</b> age, sex and answers to up to 223 questions (yes/no, scales from 0 to 10, multiple choice).</li>
  <li><b>Output:</b> a <span class="g">differential diagnosis</span> over 49 diseases, one of four <span class="g">triage</span>
    levels, and recommended next steps.</li>
  <li><b>Headline result:</b> {{ es1 }} of {{ n_test }} test patients get the right disease ranked first, and every
    emergency is triaged as an emergency.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Why an expert system?</b> A machine-learning classifier can be accurate, but it cannot say which facts and which
  guideline support its answer, so a clinician cannot check it. An expert system explains by construction: its
  reasoning is a chain of rules a person can read. The assignment asks for exactly that: an ontology and knowledge
  graph, IF–THEN rules with references, and reasoning that derives new conclusions and explains them.</p>
</section>

<!-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">2</span>The data: DDXPlus</h2>
<p class="lead">DDXPlus (NeurIPS 2022) is a public benchmark for automatic diagnosis: a knowledge base of 49 diseases
  reviewed by physicians, and about 1.3 million synthetic patients generated from it.</p>
{{ analogy("A flight simulator for diagnosis. The patients are realistic and plentiful, so a system can be tested
  safely and at scale, and every case comes with the right answer. But a simulator is not the sky: real patients are
  the next test.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <div class="split">
    <div style="flex:1025602;background:#9AAAB9">training split, about 1.0 million: not used, DDX-ES is not trained</div>
    <div style="flex:132448;background:var(--blue)">validation</div>
    <div style="flex:134529;background:var(--green)">test</div>
  </div>
  <div class="split-labels">
    <div><i style="background:#9AAAB9"></i><b>Training, about 1.0 million:</b> not used. DDX-ES is not trained; its
      knowledge comes from the expert knowledge base and the published guidelines.</div>
    <div><i style="background:var(--blue)"></i><b>Validation, {{ n_val }}:</b> counted to calibrate how strongly each
      finding points to each disease.</div>
    <div><i style="background:var(--green)"></i><b>Test, {{ n_test }}:</b> kept sealed and used only for the
      evaluation.</div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Figure 2. How the 1.3 million DDXPlus patients are used (bar widths to scale). A further sample of
    {{ sample }} test patients is stored in the graph for demonstrations and the simulated consultations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Each patient record holds age, sex, the answers to the questionnaire, the true disease and a reference
  differential. The questions are of three kinds: yes/no, scales from 0 to 10 (such as pain intensity) and multiple
  choice (such as pain location). There are 110 symptom questions and 113 <span class="g">antecedent</span> questions
  about risk factors and history.</p>
<p>The test split was kept sealed while the system was built: only the validation split was used to count how often
  each finding appears with each disease. One caution shapes the whole evaluation: the patients were generated by a
  rule-based program from the same knowledge base, so their statistics describe that knowledge base, not real
  clinical practice (section 14).</p>
</section>

<!-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">3</span>Where the knowledge comes from</h2>
<p class="lead">DDX-ES combines three sources of knowledge. Each does a different job, and all three end up in the
  same knowledge graph.</p>
{{ analogy("A doctor's training has three parts: the textbook says what each disease looks like, the guidelines say
  how to decide, and experience says how often a sign really means a disease. DDX-ES has one of each.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <div class="flow" style="gap:8pt">
    <div class="node"><b>1 · The textbook</b>The DDXPlus expert knowledge base: which symptoms and risk factors each of
      the 49 diseases can produce ({{ kb_links }} links), its severity from 1 to 5 and its ICD-10 chapter.</div>
    <div class="node cream"><b>2 · The guidelines</b>{{ n_sources }} published sources ({{ mix }}) turned into
      {{ n_rules }} IF–THEN rules, each citing its source.</div>
    <div class="node green"><b>3 · Experience</b>Counts from {{ n_val }} validation patients: a likelihood ratio for
      each of the {{ pairs }} (finding, disease) pairs of the knowledge base.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-around;color:var(--darkgold)">{{ arr("down gold") }}{{ arr("down gold") }}{{ arr("down gold") }}</div>
  <div class="node blue" style="text-align:center"><b>Knowledge graph</b>build_kg.py writes six Turtle files with
    {{ asserted }} triples; the OWL 2 RL reasoner adds {{ inferred }} more</div>
  <figcaption>Figure 3. Three sources, one graph.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The published sources include the Wells score for pulmonary embolism, the Sampson criteria for anaphylaxis, CRB-65
  for pneumonia, Centor and IDSA for sore throat, WHO and CDC case definitions, NICE NG12 for suspected cancer,
  DSM-5, ICHD-3, and the ESC, GOLD and GINA guidelines. Every DOI was checked against Crossref.</p>
<p>A published criterion does not always fit the questionnaire. The <span class="g">mapping fidelity</span> of each rule
  records how well it does: {{ fid['faithful'] }} rules are <b>faithful</b> (every item has a question),
  {{ fid['partial'] }} are <b>partial</b> (some items cannot be asked, such as heart rate in the Wells score) and
  {{ fid['proxy'] }} are <b>proxies</b> (an item is approximated by a related question).</p>
</section>

<!-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">4</span>The knowledge graph</h2>
<p class="lead">Everything DDX-ES knows is stored as one graph of small facts, <span class="g">RDF triples</span>
  (subject, predicate, object), described by a hand-written <span class="g">ontology</span> in OWL 2.</p>
{{ analogy("A library catalogue. Every disease, question, rule and source has a card, and the cards point to each
  other: this rule needs that symptom, concludes this disease and comes from that guideline. The OWL 2 RL reasoner is
  the librarian who adds the cross-references nobody wrote by hand.") }}
</div>
