<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">6</span>The inference engine</h2>
<p class="lead">One run of the engine over a patient has four phases.</p>
{{ analogy("A detective. First collect the clues, then rule out the suspects who could not have done it, then weigh
  the evidence against those who remain, and finally decide how urgently to act.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <div class="phases">
    <div class="phase"><span class="d">1 · Collect the clues</span><b class="t">Forward chaining</b>The abstraction,
      scoring and diagnostic rules fire in strata, adding concepts, scores and evidence for diseases.
      <div class="stat">about {{ fired }} rules fire per patient</div></div>
    <div class="phase"><span class="d">2 · Rule out suspects</span><b class="t">Abduction by set covering</b>A disease
      stays a candidate only if its profile explains every reported symptom.
      <div class="stat">{{ cands }} of 49 diseases remain on average; one for {{ single }} of patients</div></div>
    <div class="phase"><span class="d">3 · Weigh the evidence</span><b class="t">Belief aggregation</b>Rule certainty
      factors and the calibrated likelihood ratios are added up as log-odds with the prior, then normalised.
      <div class="stat">a probability for every candidate</div></div>
    <div class="phase"><span class="d">4 · Decide how urgent</span><b class="t">Triage and advice</b>Triage rules and the
      severity of likely diseases propose levels; the most urgent wins. Recommendation rules add next steps.
      <div class="stat">see section 8</div></div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Figure 6. The four phases of one inference run.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><span class="g">Forward chaining</span> works like a production system: rules whose conditions hold are put on an
  agenda, ordered by salience, specificity and rule order, and fired once each. Every condition is <b>true</b>,
  <b>false</b> or <b>unknown</b>. For a complete patient record an unasked question counts as “no”; in a live
  consultation it stays unknown. That lets the same engine work on records and on interviews.</p>
<p><span class="g">Set covering</span> (Reggia and colleagues, 1983) is a form of <span class="g">abduction</span>,
  reasoning from effects back to causes. It is what makes the ranking sharp: for most patients only one or two
  diseases can explain everything that was reported.</p>
</section>

