<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">7</span>Certainty factors</h2>
<p class="lead">A <span class="g">certainty factor</span> (CF), from −1 to +1, says how much a piece of evidence changes
  belief. DDX-ES reads CFs as <span class="g">likelihood ratios</span>, which made it both more accurate and easier to
  explain.</p>
{{ analogy("Betting odds. A bookmaker updates the odds with every new clue: a CF of 0.5 doubles the odds of a
  disease, a CF of 0.9 multiplies them by ten, and a CF of −0.5 halves them. Keeping score in log-odds is like keeping
  points on a scoreboard: each clue adds or subtracts points, and there is no ceiling.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <div class="scale">
    <div style="background:#F6D3CF"><div class="cf">−0.9</div><div class="x">odds ×0.1</div></div>
    <div style="background:#FBE4E1"><div class="cf">−0.5</div><div class="x">odds ×0.5</div></div>
    <div style="background:var(--codebg)"><div class="cf">0</div><div class="x">no change</div></div>
    <div style="background:#E9F4EC"><div class="cf">+0.5</div><div class="x">odds ×2</div></div>
    <div style="background:#D9EDDF"><div class="cf">+0.7</div><div class="x">odds ×3.3</div></div>
    <div style="background:#C7E4CF"><div class="cf">+0.9</div><div class="x">odds ×10</div></div>
    <div style="background:#B3DBBE"><div class="cf">+0.99</div><div class="x">odds ×100</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow" style="margin-top:10pt">
    <div class="node white"><b>Before</b>prior 2%<br>odds 1 : 49</div>{{ arr() }}
    <div class="node green"><b>Clue A, CF 0.7</b>odds × 3.3</div>{{ arr() }}
    <div class="node green"><b>Clue B, CF 0.5</b>odds × 2</div>{{ arr() }}
    <div class="node blue"><b>After</b>12%<br>odds 1 : 7.4</div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Figure 7. Top: what a certainty factor does to the odds of a disease. Bottom: a worked example with
    made-up numbers; in log-odds the two clues simply add.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>MYCIN combined two positive CFs with the formula below. Heckerman (1986) showed that a CF can be read as a
  transformed likelihood ratio λ, and that this formula then multiplies the likelihood ratios:</p>
<div class="formula">
  <div><span class="muted small">MYCIN:</span> combine(a, b) = a + b (1 − a)</div>
  <div><span class="muted small">Heckerman:</span> λ = 1 / (1 − CF) for CF ≥ 0, and λ = 1 + CF for CF &lt; 0</div>
  <div><span class="muted small">DDX-ES:</span> log-odds after = log-odds before + Σ log λ</div>
</div>
<p><b>Why it mattered.</b> With a few dozen strong clues, the classic formula reaches CF 1.0 for several diseases at
  once, and then nothing can separate them. Stable and unstable angina both reached 1.0; the prior decided, and every
  stable-angina patient in the demonstration sample was called unstable angina. Doing the same calculation as a sum of
  log likelihood ratios, the scoreboard of the analogy, removed the problem.</p>
<div class="tiles t2" style="max-width:60%">
  <div class="tile red"><div class="num">96.9%</div><div class="lab">top-1 with the classic combination (demonstration
    patients)</div></div>
  <div class="tile green"><div class="num">99.3%</div><div class="lab">top-1 with the sum of log likelihood ratios</div></div>
</div>
</section>

