<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">11</span>Provenance: the graph remembers why</h2>
<p class="lead">Every conclusion is written back into the knowledge graph with the way it was derived, in the W3C
  vocabulary <span class="g">PROV-O</span>.</p>
{{ analogy("A receipt. It records what was concluded, by which rule, from which facts, and citing which guideline, so
  anyone can check it later without running the system again.") }}
</div>
<figure>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 600 262" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Provenance chain">
    <defs><marker id="ap" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M0 1L9 5L0 9z" fill="#01416B"/></marker></defs>
    <g font-size="10.5">
      <rect x="0" y="0" width="330" height="48" rx="8" fill="#FFF4CC" stroke="#C99A00"/>
      <text x="14" y="20" font-weight="700" fill="#8A6100" font-size="11.5">:Inference (a triage inference)</text>
      <text x="14" y="37" fill="#1F2D3A">inferred triage: Emergency · about :Patient_T002456</text>
      <rect x="0" y="72" width="330" height="48" rx="8" fill="#E6EEF5" stroke="#C9D6E3"/>
      <text x="14" y="92" font-weight="700" fill="#01416B" font-size="11.5">:RuleFiring (a prov:Activity)</text>
      <text x="14" y="109" fill="#1F2D3A">which rule fired, on which facts and beliefs, at which step</text>
      <rect x="0" y="144" width="330" height="48" rx="8" fill="#E6EEF5" stroke="#C9D6E3"/>
      <text x="14" y="164" font-weight="700" fill="#01416B" font-size="11.5">:Rule_T-PE (entailed: a :RedFlagRule)</text>
      <text x="14" y="181" fill="#1F2D3A">IF P(pulmonary embolism) ≥ 0.5 THEN Emergency</text>
      <rect x="0" y="214" width="330" height="48" rx="8" fill="#E3F2E8" stroke="#BFDCC8"/>
      <text x="14" y="234" font-weight="700" fill="#1E6E3E" font-size="11.5">:Src_konstantinides2020</text>
      <text x="14" y="251" fill="#1F2D3A">2019 ESC Guidelines on acute pulmonary embolism · DOI</text>
      <rect x="410" y="66" width="190" height="60" rx="8" fill="#FFF4CC" stroke="#C99A00"/>
      <text x="424" y="86" font-weight="700" fill="#8A6100" font-size="11.5">:Inference</text>
      <text x="424" y="102" fill="#1F2D3A">belief: pulmonary embolism</text>
      <text x="424" y="117" fill="#1F2D3A">P = 1.00</text>
    </g>
    <g stroke="#01416B" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none">
      <path d="M40 48V70" marker-end="url(#ap)"/><path d="M40 120V142" marker-end="url(#ap)"/>
      <path d="M40 192V212" marker-end="url(#ap)"/><path d="M330 96H408" marker-end="url(#ap)"/>
    </g>
    <g font-family="'IBM Plex Mono', monospace" font-size="9.5" fill="#01416B">
      <text x="50" y="64">prov:wasGeneratedBy (the inference came from this firing)</text>
      <text x="50" y="136">:firedRule</text>
      <text x="50" y="207">dct:source</text>
      <text x="340" y="90">prov:used</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>Figure 13. The provenance of one conclusion: patient T002456 is an emergency because rule T-PE fired on the
    belief in pulmonary embolism, and T-PE encodes the 2019 ESC guidelines.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Because the record is in the graph, one SPARQL query (q08, “why is this case an emergency?”) returns the level, the
  rule, what the rule used and the citation. The web interface and the command line can export the provenance of any
  case as a Turtle file.</p>
</section>

<!-- 12 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">12</span>One patient, start to finish</h2>
<p class="lead">Patient T002456 is a pulmonary-embolism case from the test split. Here is every step, in the order the
  engine takes them.</p>
</div>
<div class="two">
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Answers.</b> Loss of consciousness, coughing up blood, shortness of breath, hormone use, active cancer,
      surgery within the last month, swollen calves.</li>
    <li><b>Abstraction.</b> Rules turn answers into concepts: signs of deep-vein thrombosis (A-DVT), recent surgery or
      immobilisation, chest pain.</li>
    <li><b>Scoring.</b> Wells score 6.5 = 3 (signs of DVT) + 1.5 (surgery) + 1 (cancer) + 1 (coughing up blood).</li>
    <li><b>Diagnosis.</b> D-PE-1 fires (Wells &gt; 4 with shortness of breath or coughing up blood, CF 0.7), and D-PE-3
      (hormone use, CF 0.2).</li>
    <li><b>Abduction.</b> Set covering keeps 1 of the 49 diseases: pulmonary embolism explains all 12 reported symptoms.
      Its probability is 1.00.</li>
    <li><b>Triage and advice.</b> T-PE proposes Emergency and overrules T-SYNCOPE (loss of consciousness: Urgent). R-PE-HIGH
      recommends CT pulmonary angiography.</li>
  </ol>
  <figure style="margin-top:0">
    <img class="framed" src="{{ fig['triage'] }}" alt="Triage card: Emergency">
    <figcaption>Figure 14. The triage card in the web interface, with the red flags that drove it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>
<p>Each of these steps is one node of the HOW proof in Figure 10, and the last one is the provenance record of
  Figure 13. Try it with <code>ddxes diagnose --patient T002456 --trace</code>.</p>
</section>

<!-- 13 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">13</span>How well it works</h2>
<p class="lead">DDX-ES was tested on all {{ n_test }} patients of the DDXPlus test split, which was never used to build
  it.</p>
{{ analogy("An exam with an answer key. Every test patient comes with the true disease, so every answer can be marked
  right or wrong, and the exam papers stayed sealed while the system was being built.") }}
</div>
<div class="tiles t4">
  <div class="tile"><div class="num">{{ es1 }}</div><div class="lab">top-1, DDX-ES, no training</div></div>
  <div class="tile grey"><div class="num">{{ nb1 }}</div><div class="lab">top-1, <span class="g">naive Bayes</span> on the
    same counts</div></div>
  <div class="tile grey"><div class="num">{{ hy1 }}</div><div class="lab">top-1, hybrid of the two</div></div>
  <div class="tile green"><div class="num">{{ emerg }}</div><div class="lab">of emergencies triaged as Emergency</div></div>
</div>
<p><span class="g">Top-1 accuracy</span> (GTPA@1) is the share of patients whose true disease is ranked first. The true
  disease is missing from the DDX-ES top three for only {{ miss3[0] }} of the {{ n_test }} patients (naive Bayes:
  {{ miss3[1] }}; hybrid: {{ miss3[2] }}). The differential score DDF1 is low ({{ ddf1 }}) by design: DDX-ES commits to
  a diagnosis, while the reference differential lists about ten diseases. A patient takes {{ ms }} ms, and
  {{ supported }} of the leading diagnoses are supported by at least one cited guideline rule.</p>
<h3>Where the accuracy comes from</h3>
<p>The <span class="g">ablation</span> switches parts of the engine off. The guideline rules alone rank the true disease
  first for only {{ ab['Guideline rules only'] }} of the patients: they describe syndromes that several diseases share
  and were never written to tell them apart. Set covering alone reaches {{ ab['Set covering only'] }}, both together
  {{ ab['Guideline rules + covering'] }}. The calibrated knowledge-base links carry the ranking:
  {{ ab['KB evidence + covering'] }} with covering. So the guideline rules earn their place elsewhere: in concepts and
  scores, triage, recommendations, and the explanations, which cite them.</p>
<figure>
  <img src="{{ fig['ablation'] }}" alt="Ablation chart" style="width:88%;margin:0 auto">
  <figcaption>Figure 15. Ablation: top-1 accuracy with parts of the engine switched off ({{ abl_n }} stratified test
    patients, up to 100 per disease).</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3>Triage safety</h3>
<div class="two">
  <figure style="margin-top:0">
    <img src="{{ fig['triage_matrix'] }}" alt="Triage confusion matrix">
    <figcaption>Figure 16. Triage on the full test split. Rows: the level implied by the true disease; columns: the level
      DDX-ES gave. Right of the diagonal is under-triage.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div>
    <p>Every emergency is triaged as an emergency, and every emergency or urgent case at least as urgent. Under-triage
      is {{ under }}; over-triage is {{ over }}, the price of the smoke alarm in section 8.</p>
    <p><b>Every rule checked.</b> All {{ n_rules }} rules were run on {{ rv_n }} held-out validation patients:
      {{ rv_fire }} of them fire, and the median precision of the diagnostic rules is {{ rv_prec }} (median lift
      {{ rv_lift }}). The weakest are broad screening criteria, such as the WHO influenza-like-illness definition, which
      are written to catch every case rather than to be precise.</p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- 14 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
<section>
<div class="intro">
<h2><span class="n">14</span>What it cannot do</h2>
<p class="lead">The results are strong, and their limits matter just as much.</p>
{{ analogy("A simulator licence is not a pilot's licence. Excellent scores on synthetic patients show that the
  reasoning works as designed, not that it is safe for real patients.") }}
</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>Synthetic data, circular by construction.</b> The patients were generated from the same knowledge base DDX-ES
    uses, so set covering is exact here. Real patients have several diseases at once, forget symptoms and answer
    inconsistently.</li>
  <li><b>Independent evidence.</b> Summing log likelihood ratios assumes the findings are independent; they are not, so
    the probabilities are overconfident.</li>
  <li><b>Mapping fidelity.</b> {{ fid['partial'] + fid['proxy'] }} rules need vital signs, tests, an examination or a
    duration that the questionnaire cannot ask.</li>
  <li><b>Certainty factors.</b> They were assigned from the sources on a fixed scale and agree only moderately with the
    precision measured on patients.</li>
  <li><b>One disease at a time.</b> Set covering looks for one disease that explains everything.</li>
</ul>
<div class="ethics"><b>Ethics.</b> DDX-ES is an educational prototype on synthetic data. It is not a medical device, it
  has not been validated with clinicians or real patients, and it must not be used for medical decisions; the
  interface says so on every page. The rules paraphrase published criteria with citations, and the clinical documents
  remain the authority.</div>
</section>

